beacond-client for Debian
==========================

beacon-status queries a beacond instance's /health endpoint:

  beacon-status                      # uses $BEACON_ENDPOINT, default http://127.0.0.1:9911
  beacon-status -e http://host:9911

Config vs. code under /etc
---------------------------

/etc/profile.d/beacon-env.sh sets the default BEACON_ENDPOINT for interactive
login shells. It is CODE, not site configuration, so it is deliberately NOT
installed as a plain file under /etc: dpkg treats every plain file under
/etc as a conffile, which means an admin's edit -- however small -- would be
preserved on every future upgrade, silently freezing that file's fixes out.

Instead, this package installs the real script at
/usr/lib/beacond/beacon-env.sh (an ordinary package file, not a conffile)
and /etc/profile.d/beacon-env.sh is a symlink to it (see
debian/beacond-client.links). A symlink is never tracked as a dpkg conffile,
so every upgrade replaces the script's actual content unconditionally --
there is no "edit it and upgrades silently stop applying fixes" trap the way
there would be for a plain file in the same location.

To customize the endpoint (or anything else) for this host, do NOT edit
beacon-env.sh -- your changes would be silently overwritten on the next
upgrade, precisely because it is no longer conffile-protected. Instead
create /etc/beacon/beacon-site.sh (an empty /etc/beacon directory is shipped
for this purpose): beacon-env.sh sources it automatically if present, and it
is entirely yours -- nothing in this package ever creates, touches, or
removes it.

  echo 'BEACON_ENDPOINT=http://beacon.internal:9911' | sudo tee /etc/beacon/beacon-site.sh

Open a new login shell afterwards so profile.d re-sources the change.

 -- beacond maintainers <beacond@example.com>
